# Test Writing Standards

## Spec File Rules

- Use test.describe and test blocks properly.
- Test cases must be independent.
- No hard waits (page.waitForTimeout(5000)).
- Use Playwright auto-waiting and locator expectations.
- Use helper functions for repeated steps.
- Keep test logic readable.

## Naming Convention

test.describe('Home Page Tests', () => {

    test('Test Case ID:1 - Verify hero section is visible', async ({ page, testData }) => {
        const homePage = new HomePage(page);
        await homePage.navigateToHome(testData);
        // test steps...
    });
  })


## Assertions

- Assertions should use Playwright's expect() API.
- Use toBeVisible(), toHaveText(), toContainText() etc.
- Avoid unnecessary console logs.

## Never:
- Put locator inside spec
- Write business logic in spec
- Mix test data directly in spec (always use fixtures)

## Never — Skipping or Focusing Tests (mandatory, enforced by ESLint + Playwright):
- Use `test.skip()` — every test case must always run without exception.
- Use `test.fixme()` — fix the underlying issue; do not suppress the test.
- Use `test.only()` — always forbidden; `forbidOnly: true` in playwright.config.ts will fail the run.
- Use `describe.skip()` or `describe.only()` — entire suites must always run.
- Use `it.skip()`, `xit()`, or `xdescribe()` — all are banned.
- Conditionally skip with `test.skip(condition, reason)` — no exceptions.
- Leave a test body empty as a way to silently pass — always add assertions.

> Enforcement: `eslint.config.mjs` raises a hard error for all of the above.
> Run `.\lint.ps1` before every commit to verify no skipped tests exist.
